<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="3-(4-hydroxyphenyl)pyruvate biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-5886" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Ubiquitous Plant Pathways (UPP) that was generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most land plants. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;https://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; The conversion of |FRAME: TYR| to |FRAME: P-HYDROXY-PHENYLPYRUVATE| by an aminotransferase is a common reaction, found in most organisms. In some organisms it is part of degradative pathways (as in |FRAME: TYRFUMCAT-PWY|) while in others it is part of biosynthetic pathways (as in |FRAME: PWY-1581|). |FRAME: P-HYDROXY-PHENYLPYRUVATE| is an central precursor, which is used for the synthesis of primary metabolites such as |FRAME: PLASTOQUINONE-9| and |FRAME: Vitamin-E|, both important quinones found in all oxygenic photosynthetic organisms, as well as secondary metabolites, such as |FRAME: CPD-6981|.</Description>
  <Authors>
    <Author name="PMN" />
    <Author name="xue" />
    <Author name="SRI" />
    <Author name="caspi" />
  </Authors>
